\markup {
  \center-column {
    \line { \vspace #1 }
    \line { "I Duetto" }
    \column {
      \fill-line { "First Movement" }
      \wordwrap {
        \bold {MM. 35-36. } Had to guess on the extent of the slur; this
        seemed to be what was intended.
      }
      \vspace #0.5
      \wordwrap {
        \bold {M. 43. } After listening to score, clear the predominat key is C major and the B should be natural.
      }
      \vspace #0.5
      \fill-line { "Second Movement" }
      \wordwrap {
        \bold {M. 16. } Added dot to correct missing time.
      }
      \vspace #0.5
      \wordwrap {
        \bold {M. 44. } Presumed the B natural carries to the B
        in next octave.
      }
      \vspace #0.5
      \wordwrap {
        \bold {M. 49. } Time missing in original, made low D a quarter note.
        Alternative would be to add half-note rest.
      }
      \vspace #0.5
      \wordwrap {
        \bold {M. 59. } Time missing in original, made C a dotted half to
        match Player One's part.
      }
    }
  }
}
\markup {
  \center-column {
    \line { \vspace  #1 }
    \line { "III Duetto" }
    \column {
      \fill-line { "First Movement"}
      \wordwrap {
        \bold {M. 47. } Changed F natural to F sharp, last sixteenth of 2nd beat,
        after listening to MIDI output. Fits F sharp in preceding and folowing measures.
      }
      \vspace #0.5
      \wordwrap {
        \bold {MM. 50-54, 127-131. } Added articulations to match Player One.
      }
      \vspace #0.5
      \wordwrap {
        \bold {M. 64. } Listening confirmed that the F sharp on first eighth note was
        assumed to hold for F an octave up on 4th eighth note
      }
      \vspace #1
      \fill-line { "Second Movement"}
      \wordwrap {
        \bold {M. 23. } Moved to key of G major, so second changed second C to C\natural.
      }
      \vspace #0.5
      \wordwrap {
        \bold {M. 48. } Google translates the text "Grazioso, without repeats".
        Replaced wih "D.C." instruction.
      }
    }
  }
}
\markup {
  \center-column {
    \line { \vspace #1 }
    \line { "IV Duetto" }
    \column {
      \column {
        \fill-line { "First Movement"}
        \wordwrap {
          \bold {M. 26. } Assumed unnecessary natural sign in source was
          meant to be a sharp.
        }
        \vspace #0.5
        \wordwrap {
          \bold {M. 29. } Changed note on first beat to E\natural. Currently
          in F major, compare to Player One at M. 37.
        }
        \vspace #0.5
        \wordwrap {
          \bold {M. 38. } B requires a natural to match current key.
        }
        \vspace #0.5
        \wordwrap {
          \bold {M. 44. } Assumed first E was intended to be \natural
          because of the E \flat accidental in 2nd beat.
        }
        \vspace #0.5
        \wordwrap {
          \bold {M. 100. } C\sharp on first beat changed to \natural on fourth beat, sounded bad without changing it.
        }
        \vspace #0.5
        \wordwrap {
          \bold {M. 114. } Decided to change second A \flat to A \natural
          to avoid ascending augmented second.
        }
        \line { \vspace #0.5 }
        \fill-line { "Second Movement"}
        \wordwrap {
          Used staccato rather than staccatissimo throughout
          since modern notation rarely uses staccatissimo
          and there are no staccatos used in the movement.
        }
        \vspace #0.5
        \wordwrap {
          \bold {MM. 69-72. } The tenor clef at the start of the system
          is clearly an error. There is no indication on previous staff
          that clef has changed, the accidentals don't work for tenor
          clef, and the resulting leaps would be uncharacteristic at this point
        }
        \line { \vspace #0.5 }
        \wordwrap {
          \bold {M. 102. } Has to be B\natural to avoid dissonance with
          player one's part; this is repetition of M. 100.
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
\markup {
  \center-column {
    \line { \vspace #1 }
    \line { "V Duetto" }
    \column {
      \fill-line { "First Movement"}
      \wordwrap {
        \bold {M. 81. } Cancelled C\sharp after first beat, listening
        to score indicates that we're in back in the key of F.
      }
      \vspace #1
      \fill-line { "Second Movement"}
      \wordwrap {
        Tried to simplify form. The two sections seem to
        flow together now but I may have altered the intent.
      }
      \vspace #0.5
      \wordwrap {
        \bold {M. 8. } Changed rest to quarter note rest to complete measure
        duration.
      }
      \vspace #0.5
      \wordwrap {
        \bold { M. 34. } I omitted a \sharp and
        a squiggle that appear in the margin.
      }
      \vspace #0.5
      \wordwrap {
        \bold { M. 21.} C \sharp accidental needs to be cancelled to
        work with other part.
      }
      \vspace #0.5
      \wordwrap {
        \bold { MM. 23-54.} B \natural seems to be intended;
        source sounds awful unless B \natural is used. Key seems to
        be either C minor or major throughout.
      }
    }
  }
}
\markup {
  \center-column {
    \line { \vspace #1 }
    \line { "VI Duetto" }
    \column {
      \wordwrap {
        The scanned Sieber edition is missing all but the first page of
        the Second Player's part for this Duet. For the remainder I
        used the score published by Gerard Billaudot in 1975.
      }
      \vspace #0.5
      \fill-line { "First Movement"}
      \wordwrap {
        \bold {MM. 3,9. } Quarter note to 1/8 note to match measure duration.
      }
      \vspace #1
      \wordwrap {
        \bold { M. 17. } The C\natural in the source clashes harshly the
        the C\sharp in Player One's part on the second beat. It looks
        like we've moved to D major so I changed this to C\sharp
        in Player Two's part.
      }
      \vspace #0.5
      \wordwrap {
        \bold { MM. 19,27. } The articulation is specified in two
        different ways
        for the 32nd notes, neither of which seems reasonable. So changed the
        slur to include the 32nd notes, with no tie on the F\sharp.
      }
      \vspace #0.5
      \wordwrap {
        \bold { MM. 33,35. } Descending E and D are \natural.
      }
      \vspace #0.5
      \wordwrap {
        \bold { M. 48.} C is \sharp, current key is D major.
      }
      \vspace #0.5
      \wordwrap {
        \bold { MM. 95,97. } Descending
        A and G are \natural. The Gerard Billaudot source used here already
        had this correction.
      }
    }
  }
}